{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 10,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.294,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "COORDINATE GEOMETRY"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.863,
            0.104,
            0.898,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "109"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.139,
            0.899,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, the point (– 4, 6) divides the line segment joining the points A(– 6, 10) and B(3, – 8) in the ratio 2 : 7."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.197,
            0.898,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "**Alternatively :** The ratio $m _ { _ 1 } : m _ { _ 2 }$ can also be written as $\\frac { m _ { 1 } } { m _ { 2 } } \\colon 1 ,$ , or k : 1. Let (– 4, 6) divide AB internally in the ratio k : 1. Using the section formula, we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.387,
            0.272,
            0.897,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "(-4,6)=\\left(\\frac{3k-6}{k+1},\\frac{-8k+10}{k+1}\\right)\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.353,
            0.135,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.426,
            0.341,
            0.565,
            0.382
          ],
          "content": "-   4 =   { \\frac { 3 k - 6 } { k + 1 } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.396,
            0.138,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.378,
            0.395,
            0.558,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "-4k-4=3k-6",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.421,
            0.139,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.433,
            0.42,
            0.508,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": "7 k = 2",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.447,
            0.139,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.415,
            0.445,
            0.538,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "k : 1 = 2 : 7",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.472,
            0.491,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": "You can check for the y-coordinate also."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.496,
            0.898,
            0.536
          ],
          "content": "So, the point (– 4, 6) divides the line segment joining the points A(– 6, 10) and B(3, – 8) in the ratio 2 : 7."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.541,
            0.899,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": "**Note :** You can also find this ratio by calculating the distances PA and PB and taking their ratios provided you know that A, P and B are collinear."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.593,
            0.901,
            0.633
          ],
          "content": "**Example 8 :** Find the coordinates of the points of trisection (i.e., points dividing in three equal parts) of the line segment joining the points A(2, – 2) and B(– 7, 4)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.639,
            0.487,
            0.703
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Let P and Q be the points of trisection of AB i.e., $\\mathrm{AP} = \\mathrm{PQ} = \\mathrm{QB}$ (see Fig. 7.11)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.547,
            0.641,
            0.842,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.65,
                0.684,
                0.744,
                0.703
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.11"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.711,
            0.9,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, P divides AB internally in the ratio 1 : 2. Therefore, the coordinates of P, by applying the section formula, are"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.763,
            0.645,
            0.807
          ],
          "content": "\\left( \\frac{1(-7)+2(2)}{1+2}, \\frac{1(4)+2(-2)}{1+2} \\right), \\mathrm{i.e.}, (-1,0)",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.821,
            0.859,
            0.841
          ],
          "content": "Now, Q also divides AB internally in the ratio 2 : 1. So, the coordinates of Q are"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.853,
            0.651,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": "\\left( \\frac{2(-7)+1(2)}{2+1}, \\frac{2(4)+1(-2)}{2+1} \\right), \\mathrm{i.e.}, (-4, 2)",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_23.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
